.TH byobu-agent-state 1 "18 November 2026" byobu "byobu"
.SH NAME
byobu-agent-state \- record and show what the coding agent in each tmux pane is doing

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBbyobu-agent-state\fP [\fB\-\-pane\fP \fI%N\fP] [\fB\-\-agent\fP \fINAME\fP] \fISTATE\fP [\fIDETAIL\fP...]
.br
\fBbyobu-agent-state\fP \fBclear\fP
.br
\fBbyobu-agent-state\fP \fB\-\-claude-hook\fP
.br
\fBbyobu-agent-state\fP \fB\-\-list\fP | \fB\-\-next\fP | \fB\-\-setup\fP
.br
\fBbyobu-agent-state\fP \fB\-\-enable-claude-hooks\fP | \fB\-\-disable-claude-hooks\fP

.SH DESCRIPTION
Coding agents (Claude Code, Codex and the like) spend much of their time waiting on you. \fBbyobu-agent-state\fP records what the agent in a tmux pane is doing, on the pane itself, so that byobu's window list can show it and tools such as Trustmux can act on it.

It also gives byobu's window list a single mark per window, after the window's name, for the most urgent thing happening in it: an agent waiting for you, a bell, new output, and so on (see \fBTHE WINDOW LIST\fP below). All of this needs tmux 3.2 or later; with older tmux, or GNU Screen, byobu's window list is unchanged, and \fBbyobu-agent-state\fP does nothing.

\fISTATE\fP is one of:
.TP
\fBworking\fP
the agent is busy: thinking, or running tools.
.TP
\fBblocked\fP
it needs you: a permission to grant, a question to answer or a choice to make.
.TP
\fBfailed\fP
it stopped on an error (an API error, a rate limit), and you haven't looked yet.
.TP
\fBdone\fP
it finished, and you haven't looked yet.
.TP
\fBidle\fP
it is waiting at its prompt, and you have seen everything it did.
.PP
\fIDETAIL\fP is a short reason, such as "approve Bash: make test"; it is shown by \fB\-\-list\fP and kept to one line of at most 80 characters. \fBclear\fP forgets the pane's agent. The pane is \fB$TMUX_PANE\fP (the one the command runs in) unless \fB\-\-pane\fP names another; \fB\-\-agent\fP names what runs there (otherwise "agent").

A done or failed agent becomes idle as soon as you look at its window: when you switch to it, or straight away if it finished in the window a terminal is showing. A control-mode client, such as Trustmux's daemon or iTerm2's tmux integration, does not count as looking.

Any agent, script or long-running job can report this way. For example, a build script might run \fBbyobu-agent-state working\fP as it starts, and \fBbyobu-agent-state done\fP or \fBbyobu-agent-state failed "3 tests failed"\fP at the end. For Claude Code, \fBbyobu-enable-agent-hooks\fP(1) installs hooks that do it for you (see \fBCLAUDE CODE\fP below).

.SH THE WINDOW LIST
Each window in byobu's window list is shown as its number, its name and one mark, with no space between them, as in:
.PP
.nf
  0:shell- 1:claude? 2:codex\[u25C9] 3:review\[u25D4] 4:build@ 5:logs!
.fi
.PP
The mark is for the most urgent thing in the window; the first of these that applies:
.TP
\fB?\fP
an agent in the window is \fBblocked\fP, waiting for you; or a bell rang in a window that holds an agent (an agent that rings the bell wants you). The window is drawn in the bell's style: bold, in byobu's accent colour.
.TP
\fB!\fP
a bell rang in the window (tmux's \fBmonitor-bell\fP). Also drawn in the accent colour.
.TP
\fB\[u2717]\fP
an agent \fBfailed\fP. Bold.
.TP
\fB\[u25C9]\fP
an agent is \fBdone\fP. Bold. (Later, a long command finishing while you were away will show this too.)
.TP
\fB@\fP
new output since you last looked (tmux's \fBmonitor-activity\fP). Bold. A window that holds an agent does not count activity: an agent prints output all the time, so byobu turns \fBmonitor-activity\fP off there until the last agent in the window clears.
.TP
\fB~\fP
no output for \fBmonitor-silence\fP seconds (off unless you turn it on).
.TP
\fB\[u25D4]\fP
an agent is \fBworking\fP.
.TP
\fB\-\fP
nothing to report: an \fBidle\fP agent, or a quiet window.
.PP
A window with a zoomed pane has its name in \fIitalics\fP. The current window is drawn in reverse video, as always.
.PP
This replaces tmux's own window flags, which it covers: \fB*\fP (the current window, already shown in reverse video), \fB\-\fP (the last window), \fB#\fP (activity, now \fB@\fP), \fB!\fP (bell, unchanged), \fB~\fP (silence, unchanged), \fBZ\fP (zoomed, now italics) and \fBM\fP (the marked pane, whose border tmux already highlights).

.SH CUSTOMIZING
Each mark is a tmux option, which you can set to any other character in \fI$BYOBU_CONFIG_DIR/.tmux.conf\fP (usually \fI~/.byobu/.tmux.conf\fP):
.PP
.nf
  set -g @byobu_mark_blocked '?'
  set -g @byobu_mark_bell '!'
  set -g @byobu_mark_failed '\[u2717]'
  set -g @byobu_mark_done '\[u25C9]'
  set -g @byobu_mark_activity '@'
  set -g @byobu_mark_silence '~'
  set -g @byobu_mark_working '\[u25D4]'
  set -g @byobu_mark_idle '\-'
.fi
.PP
Characters that some terminals draw two cells wide, such as emoji, will shift the rest of the status line in those terminals; a character missing from your terminal's font shows as a box or a question mark.
.PP
byobu replaces \fBwindow-status-format\fP and \fBwindow-status-current-format\fP only while they are tmux's defaults; if you have set your own, it is left alone. The pieces are there for your own format: \fB#{E:@byobu_window_mark}\fP is the mark and \fB#{E:@byobu_window_style}\fP the style, as in byobu's:
.PP
.nf
  #{E:@byobu_window_style}#I:#W#{E:@byobu_window_mark}
.fi
.PP
To keep tmux's classic window flags instead, set:
.PP
.nf
  set -g window-status-format '#I:#W#F '
  set -g window-status-current-format '#I:#W#F '
.fi

.SH JUMPING TO AN AGENT
\fBbyobu-agent-state \-\-next\fP switches to the pane that has needed you longest: blocked panes first, then failed, then done. Bind it to a key in \fI$BYOBU_CONFIG_DIR/keybindings.tmux\fP; for example, for the prefix (\fBCtrl-a\fP) then \fBN\fP:
.PP
.nf
  bind-key N run-shell 'byobu-agent-state \-\-next'
.fi
.PP
\fB\-\-list\fP prints every agent pane, one per line, separated by tabs: its pane ID, session:window.pane, agent, state, since (seconds since the epoch) and detail.

.SH CLAUDE CODE
\fBbyobu-enable-agent-hooks\fP(1) adds hooks to Claude Code's user settings that run \fBbyobu-agent-state \-\-claude-hook\fP, which reads each hook's event on standard input, prints nothing, and always exits successfully, so that it never gets in Claude Code's way. The events map to states as follows:
.TP
\fBSessionStart\fP
idle (and the pane's agent is "claude").
.TP
\fBUserPromptSubmit\fP, \fBPreToolUse\fP, \fBPostToolUse\fP, \fBPostToolUseFailure\fP
working.
.TP
\fBPermissionRequest\fP
blocked, with the tool and what it would run, edit or fetch as the detail.
.TP
\fBNotification\fP
blocked, for a permission prompt, a question or a form; other notifications change nothing.
.TP
\fBStop\fP
done.
.TP
\fBStopFailure\fP
failed, with the error as the detail.
.TP
\fBSessionEnd\fP
clear.

.SH TMUX OPTIONS
The state is kept in each pane's user options, which any tmux format, script or tool can read: \fB@agent\fP (claude, codex, ...), \fB@agent_state\fP, \fB@agent_since\fP (seconds since the epoch) and \fB@agent_detail\fP. For example:
.PP
.nf
  tmux list-panes \-a \-F '#{pane_id} #{@agent_state} #{@agent_detail}'
.fi
.PP
\fB\-\-setup\fP installs the window list, the marks' options (only those you haven't set) and hooks that mark a window's done and failed agents seen when it is shown; byobu runs it once per tmux server, as it starts.

.SH SEE ALSO
.PD 0
